#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147158 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4e3079d-80a6-5333-8304-7116b01de82c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Constant Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "40068330-4d79-501b-afbc-a96f8e210c37" :question/math-academy-id "e-21598" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s \\neq 0$ or $s = 0.$\n\n- If $s \\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\n- For $s > 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is convergent\n\n- For $s < 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=2$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s},$ defined for $s > 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147159 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2fd0466-9f47-5adc-911a-f297858951b1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Real Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "54b1f55e-6525-557c-9dbc-869264259455" :question/math-academy-id "e-21599" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=e^{-2t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s \\neq -2$ or $s = -2.$\n\n- If $s \\neq -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-2-s}{e}^{(-2-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>-2, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s = -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-(-2))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s \\leq -2$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s > -2$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s > -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of $f(t)=e^{-2t}$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s+2},$ defined for $s > -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147160 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a90a0c43-d87f-5639-826d-854b90876a27" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Complex Exponentials"}] :question/id #uuid "92327ab7-0ae5-54ce-92d1-a3365407dd74" :question/math-academy-id "e-21602" :question/problem "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{2\\textrm i t},$ where $t, s \\in\\mathbb  R?$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)=e^{2\\textrm i t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{2\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{2\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)= \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i} -  \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\\left[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty}e^{ -sa}\\cdot \\left(\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)\\right)  \\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s < 0,$ the term $e^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s < 0.$\n\n- For $s = 0,$ we have simply $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s = 0.$\n\n- For $s > 0,$ the term $e^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $e^{2\\textrm i t}$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i},$ defined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4e3079d-80a6-5333-8304-7116b01de82c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Constant Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd70379b-2f0e-5cad-a633-e8b8f08242d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318893} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dcd2b5-6a96-503c-88cd-2eec19b5f925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318894} {:answer/id #uuid "db9d667b-9834-5b03-ad11-307958830459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }11{e}^{st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318895} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f4d82c-1f5e-5d27-8c5d-590a358a25bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3dcd2b5-6a96-503c-88cd-2eec19b5f925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318894} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc76585-fcb5-510a-8fc3-2265fed1ca14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de541358-c5ea-5f4c-9965-328f22de7379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is convergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318898} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf3e40f-5577-5ff3-b02e-f1c83a10806f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de541358-c5ea-5f4c-9965-328f22de7379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is convergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318898} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5846dfc-2792-5d6f-8fda-8f22a67f837c" :answer-field/key "field-2"