b/ref 17592186318909} {:answer/id #uuid "98827691-b3bd-50fd-91a0-d01558ba997a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318910} {:answer/id #uuid "176a13f8-7a06-5e3b-9260-ab70df154881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11{e}^{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98827691-b3bd-50fd-91a0-d01558ba997a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318910} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68cd773-cd70-52b0-8c40-b71f423f9c4b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7381190c-3098-5bc8-8f68-aadadf04a86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318913} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d3355c-70d9-5250-aa70-db522e02c76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318914} {:answer/id #uuid "21c91dbd-eedb-5a0b-8253-16185075603a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318915} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdb23b1-0211-5ad4-a4cd-f1e24c5ef8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cdb23b1-0211-5ad4-a4cd-f1e24c5ef8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318916} :answer-field/id #uuid "37ec9162-9c7f-5f47-8a79-86ed1eca80aa" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47a81c94-08c2-5e12-a6c0-9373eb79a0d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-340712" :question/problem "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{11}=\\frac{11}{s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4e3079d-80a6-5333-8304-7116b01de82c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Constant Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "740d45de-326f-5d36-929c-aacc2ca0d453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318919} {:answer/id #uuid "890ea24a-e995-566c-8582-d0f71ba9705d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318920} {:answer/id #uuid "688edd88-bba6-5036-b730-639e3b2d4c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318921} {:answer/id #uuid "f24d27b8-0a7c-5d0b-807d-2337ad920084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{st}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "740d45de-326f-5d36-929c-aacc2ca0d453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\inf