t :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{4s-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318981} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e2de1a-16f8-552a-8c44-e511176eca44" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e260934d-7688-506d-8824-e61b4285d394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318986} {:answer/id #uuid "25f5e15a-31d2-5987-81d9-be043fc49eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318987} {:answer/id #uuid "18fd906a-dadb-528d-8957-824a14b3a140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318988} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1c22d8-449b-5253-9d44-40ecfb7f3f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318989} {:answer/id #uuid "403d5de9-6d1e-5264-abd6-ff499e8bdf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e260934d-7688-506d-8824-e61b4285d394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318986} :answer-field/id #uuid "99822e3c-19a4-52f9-8caf-6b695cfe6ec8" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93c3f738-7e1b-55e4-9a09-822a44f91527" :question/math-academy-id "q-340731" :question/problem "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/4}.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-2}}\nthe integral is convergent.\n\n$\\,$ For {{field-3}}\nthe integral is divergent.\n\nEvaluating our integral in the convergent case, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{{e}^{t/4}}=\\frac{4}{4s-1},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{t/4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st+t/4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ or $s=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- If $s\\ne \\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{1/4-s}{e}^{(1/4-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1/4-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{1}{4}) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(1/4-1/4)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>\\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is convergent.\n\n- For $s\\le \\frac{1}{4}$ the integral is divergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s>\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(1/4-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4}-\\frac{1}{s-1/4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{t/4}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{4}{4s-1},$ defined for $s>\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a90a0c43-d87f-5639-826d-854b90876a27" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Complex Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c150e6e2-86b2-55cc-9390-294cfde0547c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{s+4\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318993} {:answer/id #uuid "a89dfc93-4478-5cff-9742-e39243fd71f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318994} {:answer/id #uuid "85bad7ff-8550-5193-94c6-6c6ad16a4bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{s-4\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318995} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef09a7b-4560-5299-ad2c-636ac4b60bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef09a7b-4560-5299-ad2c-636ac4b60bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318996} :answer-field/id #uuid "11882a6e-0ac3-5f4b-9c3c-0e2c386b6eee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132b0326-b01f-51f2-9dd4-292851cf9ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318998} {:answer/id #uuid "235c7ff3-db7f-5028-8184-84cc717882aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318999} {:answer/id #uuid "bb408b50-221c-50ff-b155-05f68d4c0fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319000} {:answer/id #uuid "677d781f-27a0-5657-b3f8-f44b7d3c12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "677d781f-27a0-5657-b3f8-f44b7d3c12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319001} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe1e4ce-4154-576e-8834-9ffc882766f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4efd2036-c489-52bf-89f9-36ddc55c16f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-340736" :question/problem "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4\\text{i}t},$ where $t,s\\in R?$$\\,L(f(t))=$\n{{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{4\\text{i}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{4\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\text{i}-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(4\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{4\\text{i}a}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4a)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4a))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{4\\text{i}t}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a90a0c43-d87f-5639-826d-854b90876a27" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Complex Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid 