wer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1ffe212-8036-586e-ac88-2666ab9a48c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340748" :question/problem "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8},$ where $t,s\\in R?$$\\,L(f(t))=$\n{{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-3\\text{i}/8-s}{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-3\\text{i}/8-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3\\text{i}/8-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-3\\text{i}a/8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a90a0c43-d87f-5639-826d-854b90876a27" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Laplace Transforms of Complex Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df2ea3d3-2e27-5f56-95fe-4b873df13572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319037} {:answer/id #uuid "7fefdf6b-9bd0-5457-b366-7e8bbefd7d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9s+\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319038} {:answer/id #uuid "137c0ef0-8f85-532b-9a0d-ed8594de83a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9s-\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319039} {:answer/id #uuid "458cdaa7-547b-554b-979a-9e45e8dfd46a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{9s-\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2ea3d3-2e27-5f56-95fe-4b873df13572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319037} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c36329f-63da-58b5-8ab9-1dc6fd893176" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1082ca43-7a9d-5561-aca8-ec05b018c937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>1/9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319042} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3dd45c-34fe-5c2f-b0cf-9dab3738b98b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319043} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcf5720-2452-5e5b-bbdf-e105d3ef8620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-1/9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319044} {:answer/id #uuid "e61b6af5-4365-58f4-9060-d611d9ddd445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e61b6af5-4365-58f4-9060-d611d9ddd445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319045} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f4f1a7b-6b4f-5cc8-b7b3-a4e9da506b6d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "703dd62d-f796-5fe7-af4e-b3a082f0ecec" :question/math-academy-id "q-340750" :question/problem "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-\\text{i}t/9},$ where $t,s\\in R?$$\\,L(f(t))=$\n{{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-\\text{i}t/9},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-\\text{i}a/9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"}]}
